    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d231">

                <pb facs="0362" n="344" sameAs="frus:frus1946v05/pg344"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.00119 EW/5–2946: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the Ambassador in the Soviet
                        Union</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Smith</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left"><hi rendition="#small-caps">restricted</hi></seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1946-05-29T20:00:00-04:00">May 29, 1946—8 p.m.</date></dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>993. The following message from the Secretary to Molotov should accompany the
                    draft treaty for Austria: <quote rend="blockquote">
                        <p>“In Paris you pointed out that American memorandum on the Austrian treaty
                            was not in form of a draft treaty.<anchor n="13" xml:id="d231-f1"/> I
                            have accordingly put our suggestions in form of a draft treaty for your
                            consideration. You will note that the draft is complete except for
                            certain clauses which will be virtually identical with whatever
                            provisions on the same subject matter we agreed to insert in the
                                Italian<anchor n="14" xml:id="d231-f2"/> and Balkan treaties.<anchor
                                n="15" xml:id="d231-f3"/> The draft is neither long nor complicated.
                            I hope very much that you will take the opportunity to examine the draft
                            before our meeting in Paris on June 15 and that you will agree to a
                            discussion of the Austrian treaty at that time. I am sending copies of
                            the draft to Mr. Bevin<anchor n="16" xml:id="d231-f4"/> and Mr.
                                Bidault<anchor n="17" xml:id="d231-f5"/> and also asking their
                            agreement to a discussion of the Austrian treaty at our meeting.”</p>
                    </quote></p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Byrnes</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d231-f1" n="13" xml:id="d231-f1-content">
                <p>See volume <hi rendition="#small-caps">ii</hi>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d231-f2" n="14" xml:id="d231-f2-content">
                <p>For text of Italian treaty, see Department of State, Treaties and Other
                    International Acts Series No. 1648, or 61 Stat. (pt. 2) 1245.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d231-f3" n="15" xml:id="d231-f3-content">
                <p>For texts of these treaties, see TIAS Nos. 1649, 1650, and 1651, or 61 Stat. (pt.
                    2) 1757, 1915, and 2065.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d231-f4" n="16" xml:id="d231-f4-content">
                <p>Ernest Bevin, British Secretary of State for Foreign Affairs.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d231-f5" n="17" xml:id="d231-f5-content">
                <p>Georges Bidault, French Minister for Foreign Affairs.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
